  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n05Z synopsis shows a band of weak storms in southeast Colorado and a \nsubsequent upper level wave which is providing the lift for the \nstorms.  Storms are also trying to develop in northeast Colorado \nwith another upper level wave which will move into western Kansas \nthrough the overnight.\n\n\nTuesday the day should start with decaying storms along an upper \nlevel wave that enters into central Kansas towards late morning. \nResidual cloud cover along with post storm winds coming out of the \nnorth should keep temperatures at bay through the morning.  By \nafternoon skies will clear and daytime heating along with rapid \ndestabilization of the atmosphere will combine with a shortwave \nmoving out of Nebraska with the northwest flow to develop storms in \nnorthwest and north central Kansas.  A second shortwave in southeast \nColorado should also access the destabilized atmosphere and another \narea of convection in Colorado will develop and move into western \nKansas by evening.  CAPE values are expected to be higher with the \nwestern line of storms and short term models are hinting at cold \npooling with the storms and the production of 60-70 mph gusts.  00Z \nskew t forecasts from various short term models suggest around 30 \nkts of bulk shear which would support some hail production of around \nquarter size but with more north to northwest flow in the mid levels \nthe hodographs aren't really showing a large to giant hail \nenvironment.  Localized heavy rainfall is also possible as PWAT \nvalues of 1.5 inches looks to be common across western Kansas and \nNBM probabilities of greater than 1 inch of rain range from 10-20% \nacross western Kansas suggesting some of the storms should drop \nquite a bit of rain in a short amount of time.  With continuous \nnorthwest flow through the night and another upper level wave moving \nin from northeast Colorado short term models suggest a 3rd round of \nstorms moving from northwest to north central Kansas by sunrise \nWednesday morning.\n\nFor Wednesday the cooling trend should continue as the 597 dm upper \nlevel high retreats to the west and the surface winds are more \neasterly.  NBM 25th-75th percentile spread for highs range from mid \n80s to lower 90s for much of western Kansas.  Leftover morning \nconvection should keep clouds in place through the morning but by \nafternoon we should be back to full sunshine and destabilization of \nthe atmosphere.  Another round of convection should develop in the \nfront range but given the more northerly flow in the mid levels \nthese storms will have a longer and harder time trying to make it to \nwestern Kansas.  Areas along the Colorado border will have the \nhighest probabilities (40-50%) of rain after midnight.\n\nEnsemble trends show the upper high moving east towards the weekend \nbringing back in hotter air and NBM probabilities of 100 degree or \nhotter highs is back to 30-50% on Saturday and 40-80% on Sunday.",
